#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "c7f85c6d-a7d6-5ae6-b82b-7e8608b6411e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21757" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/270055d134c778004d3e31c76b700bd57c62f1cde2cd103356fef63b2be3ff9e.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t = 0\\,\\textrm s$ is $1\\,\\textrm{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $12\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 12\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 2\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 2\\cdot\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4 = 12I + 2\\cdot\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I = 2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $I_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 6\\alpha = 2\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $I_p(t) = \\dfrac13.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-6t} + \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\textrm{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0) = 1,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-6(0)}+\\frac{1}{3} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{-6t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c = \\dfrac23e^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $I_p = \\dfrac13$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "9be480e2-68b0-591c-b331-0751a225122c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21758" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/e3ab0152f1898f25b6fbf431ba398be01af8f9dad6664f27c28ea7549bff18e5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t) = 7\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $14\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $7\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 14\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 14I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 7\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThe source voltage (EMF) is given by\n\n$$\nE(t) = 7\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n7\\sin(3t) = 14I + 7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 7\\sin(3t) - 14I,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\sin(3t) - 2I,\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2I = \\sin(3t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "2594ae82-9bd8-5ee4-a5a9-4be27b5d9097" :question/math-academy-id "e-21761" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/6729737d837bef39687106d530899d797dd69c54c92c2d31c2afd99405bc03e5.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, **rounded to two decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 5\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\sin(3t) = 5I + \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = 5\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(3t) + \\beta\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +5\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{15}{34},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{25}{34}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{25}{34}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.86\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b3dbc63-e041-5954-b252-bcf621450f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b3dbc63-e041-5954-b252-bcf621450f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320712} :answer-field/id #uuid "390e5b47-3803-5209-a661-06e32cd98311" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13747a80-1def-5154-809e-6e4568d44dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-336210" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/9f7c87d6914b44c303fa7c67c9f5bb4908cf3e30912886d5945eddb899549032.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=9I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+9{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(9\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +9\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 9\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{15})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}}≈0.84\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db3c4f7-03b7-57ad-aa7b-c329ac6f1625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320715} {:answer/id #uuid "aa70ffd8-c368-54c6-81f7-26dbe8b7688e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320716} {:answer/id #uuid "f36855c0-ec78-5eda-aef0-9a855f752f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320717} {:answer/id #uuid "2621019d-3da3-5e33-bb32-277064c4a971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320718} {:answer/id #uuid "df48ce28-d9cf-59cb-adee-f47165ef989f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-18I=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2621019d-3da3-5e33-bb32-277064c4a971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320718} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b8cf85-a8f5-5221-8a53-db19ef2c1c3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5db08a12-2bed-5c19-bee6-fdb879966b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336533" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds)." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827e4220-59ff-5c83-8306-b566d1ce4de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-36I=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320722} {:answer/id #uuid "d627d9f0-204a-531f-8805-68680862a09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320723} {:answer/id #uuid "02f0943c-4a97-5f53-960f-d04c2faac2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+36I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320724} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbac54b-44a5-55e4-9afc-620e562836b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320725} {:answer/id #uuid "32199242-3d24-5b7b-9132-2d3c274c1c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-12I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dbac54b-44a5-55e4-9afc-620e562836b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320725} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24fc794-a4e6-54fc-afa1-f0def259ba59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f29be7ed-647b-58a7-96bd-11c7ff2100a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336534" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/c644931a5d7c3b015e00518269920718cde39882b45d3589e12e6b90d0391d46.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $36\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=36\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=36I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=36I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8ea85da-aa8c-5c1f-be7e-96677cc036d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320729} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aec0c5-ec0f-5223-8afb-f6103f23b03a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320730} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1da50b-0f87-55ec-a82f-32d041402416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-6I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320731} {:answer/id #uuid "0a57f289-f7fd-5130-942b-a9e4357c4cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320732} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0d9e3b-e787-5c7e-afb7-f12a2813e7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-18I=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7aec0c5-ec0f-5223-8afb-f6103f23b03a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320730} :answer-field/id #uuid "a45663fc-5937-595a-a5fd-97ef52a854de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c567c077-7ad2-5d46-9b68-9f12997144df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336542" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/91c132c39e9dedeb5377d850a344e555ba0755c3fd6183220674cb7f967e943b.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884a5272-27a3-5d57-9466-5e634f196ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320736} {:answer/id #uuid "37a43329-d36d-5ae7-bdea-82c73bf260a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-4I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320737} {:answer/id #uuid "f042ea15-2818-58cc-81c9-f8d490497d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+16I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320738} {:answer/id #uuid "599fbe0b-d5c3-565f-b34c-8ea39ab9fe92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320739} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd4f528-0d65-5038-83d1-f281ed18cac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-16I=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "884a5272-27a3-5d57-9466-5e634f196ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320736} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ef705c0-5061-50c3-9b0e-24c2f354e45a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4d839e7-a50b-5e3c-807d-d3f25cbe32f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336543" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/a7005971c36efa45c4e8c3f752bdc4aec5af3a8b92734e83e85470d10bfbc299.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f927f6-5546-5042-a7a2-7f2fcbed89a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320743} {:answer/id #uuid "48ac7453-72f7-56c7-8a3e-2fdcbb34cdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-9I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320744} {:answer/id #uuid "303ff0a0-bb18-51b4-b5f5-072e815529b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320745} {:answer/id #uuid "a2693c51-9dd7-5198-9998-228ef866fb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320746} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c37afc-a0a4-5da0-8af0-e8c68c2ef928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2693c51-9dd7-5198-9998-228ef866fb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dfe6fcf-ee28-5baa-beef-def0b1219075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38df6533-3590-5853-a74e-af1a3c2be976" :question/math-academy-id "q-336546" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/999acaa0bc014b7afc7c916dd03c957e15707ca680836341e5a071a1f321e97d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=9I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3511226-8155-58e2-a084-e9e4cd6ddd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320750} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d116bb-35fe-5a58-aabe-a6c545478ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3511226-8155-58e2-a084-e9e4cd6ddd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320750} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f43c4a-9e39-5a07-b318-4ab2942a6e77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "966610e5-12bf-5626-82d0-fa1440106fe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336643" :question/problem "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{1}{2}$ amperes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1072e537-fd96-5ba7-af06-d2a1ac235901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1072e537-fd96-5ba7-af06-d2a1ac235901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320754} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bfec7e-3d44-5e60-84cf-4f3b9d7df94d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96381f71-092f-5f0d-97d3-2b7abf80a94b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336645" :question/problem "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=5,\\,I(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{5}{8}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{5}{8}$ amperes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a199c7-28ad-56ac-bd99-43440c315095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}e^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21a199c7-28ad-56ac-bd99-43440c315095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}e^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320757} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b510e44-f7db-5f3b-bb10-be17b7b59bae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "040a7edf-5361-5524-9d07-997422bb5a36" :question/math-academy-id "q-336649" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/7e7a2252fdab7a363557a056c58b10f228a13b4aa2029b6b20867a95e5cdde79.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5=25I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=5.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+25{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n${I}_{p}=\\alpha .$ \nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n \n\n$$\n0+25\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-25t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{5}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496db15c-a15f-5d90-b84d-32458421475e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-8t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320760} {:answer/id #uuid "2c804ffd-1693-5edb-bf5f-d445b32c2dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c804ffd-1693-5edb-bf5f-d445b32c2dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320761} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5bba320-3b60-538e-9a6a-d823313d1e54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "024bf0a5-9232-5172-b017-5423dd2d58c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336651" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/62dc1d019806908359e3956386f40c1a3ca53d720db7ba0c3122d8eb4ba37c2e.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}={e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b97f606-25c8-5338-9bcb-8c92c6316db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b97f606-25c8-5338-9bcb-8c92c6316db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320764} :answer-field/id #uuid "07fd7a77-b510-5e5c-ade5-9a2685173960" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a30b42d-5dac-58ed-9184-342224e2507d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336653" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.03$ seconds given that the initial current is $3\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.03$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4=16I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $3\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =3 \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(11{e}^{-8t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.03$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.03)=\\frac{1}{4}(11{e}^{-8(0.03)}+1)≈2.41\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73ba4738-a53b-5bff-9adf-46bb23b8fa19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320767} {:answer/id #uuid "7add713f-8ae3-5433-8a1a-71f178fe6c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320768} {:answer/id #uuid "d069d928-bde3-5730-b5a2-d563a894f7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320769} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa76b02-32fd-5209-967e-007ac63e9eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320770} {:answer/id #uuid "b2be1a5d-a412-5433-a6c0-15cae30aa10a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2be1a5d-a412-5433-a6c0-15cae30aa10a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320771} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f6eae3-68fb-5e70-9c87-0f6c74a755cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a736dc41-749c-5028-a4ef-9266d080992e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336656" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/73633edf3781ad5748b02695af69a9f3b047909d62bccb4a88a47a9f8d9de03f.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=8I+1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(8\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +8\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{73},$ $\\beta =\\frac{32}{73}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{12}{73}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{32}{73}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{12}{73}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{32}{73}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{73},\\,\\beta =\\frac{32}{73}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{12}{73}+\\frac{32}{73} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a595682d-25fa-536e-b935-cb619b44bdd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320774} {:answer/id #uuid "e418ecea-4451-55e2-a7b3-de3c6879609c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320775} {:answer/id #uuid "3057d0a3-127f-5b4d-a686-5183d9d33952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320776} {:answer/id #uuid "c6db0bda-ca22-5eac-acd5-8a8cbf05abd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320777} {:answer/id #uuid "240f87a9-d8c2-59ff-83d1-9a268d4d9bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6db0bda-ca22-5eac-acd5-8a8cbf05abd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320777} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbb824f-d217-5e9e-a011-f9e9fc6e69d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd7101c0-4e44-5fde-a3ab-5c37fe0cf2cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-336740" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/7f997f7647886e1eea24d292ddd66b2d331d6f7d4c7ffe47b6a52b52dd029a44.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=12I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8abe6c0a-c202-54cf-9d78-faba28917045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320781} {:answer/id #uuid "963b131e-a27b-547b-b1d7-daf8e4c5a556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320782} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb54cd5-2ffb-5ea7-9d2f-cff3f7085478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320783} {:answer/id #uuid "72ecfd68-5eca-50ba-9873-9ce29212d0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320784} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb868b8-a4c0-527c-a2b4-548944a20142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb868b8-a4c0-527c-a2b4-548944a20142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320785} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b0c1b2-8503-53ed-a8d9-7a097ae42c51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac3bde59-e38c-53e2-a951-f1df90284fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336742" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/e08922ed1028b9fa5b799d52d25aa6b5e23e71cfa1cbd4f152ac592422d68f8d.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=12I+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{8}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{2}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{17},\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{17}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{17}+\\frac{8}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1abc16-30f0-5bb9-9d9f-e7248b26399d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320788} {:answer/id #uuid "feca3506-ce06-53d2-8c8c-5347636f2241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feca3506-ce06-53d2-8c8c-5347636f2241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320789} :answer-field/id #uuid "98e0efb8-3d28-5b5c-80ef-9c0cb51aaef8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3bbc417-0d98-5834-93f1-6f63f2deaba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336751" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/4b304da0cca6e678fd53931c6949ff51546486df844553e79608542a8bf7886d.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=10I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{15}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{29})}^{2}+{(\\frac{15}{29})}^{2}}≈0.56\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
